EXAMPLES

Example 1 — Discovering and using a domain tool

The agent searches for a tool, then calls it programmatically inside `execute_{server}_code`:

[Agent makes tool call: search_tools with query="optimal power flow"]

search_tools returns: run_opf on server powergrid, with parameters network_path (str, required).

[Agent makes tool call: execute_powergrid_code with code="""
result = run_opf(network_path="<blob>aHR0cHM6Ly8...uYw2</blob>")
print(f"OPF success: {result['success']}")
print(f"Solver status: {result['status']}")
objective = result['objective']
if objective is not None:
    print(f"Objective value: {objective:.2f}")
"""]

After the tool returns successfully:
{
  "explanation": "The power flow analysis completed successfully. The network has 5 buses with total load of 150 MW.",
  "response": {
    "action": "solution",
    "solution": "Power flow analysis complete. Key findings:\n1. All buses within voltage limits\n2. No line overloads detected\n3. Total generation: 155 MW",
    "provenance": "Used run_opf via execute_powergrid_code"
  },
  "plan": "1. [✓] Search for power flow tool\n2. [✓] Run optimal power flow\n3. [✓] Summarize results",
  "status": "Analysis complete"
}

Example 2 — Chaining multiple tool calls in one execution

The agent chains tool calls in a single `execute_{server}_code` invocation:

[Agent makes tool call: execute_powergrid_code with code="""
# Run OPF — the network is loaded implicitly from the path
opf_result = run_opf(network_path="<blob>aHR0cHM6Ly8...uYw2</blob>")
print(f"OPF success: {opf_result['success']}")

# The returned network object gives access to components (only if successful)
if opf_result['success']:
    network = opf_result['network']
    print(f"Buses: {len(network.buses)}")
    print(f"Lines: {len(network.lines)}")
    print(f"Generators: {len(network.generators)}")
"""]
